  "summary": "A digital art style using pixelated outlines to define forms within limited resolution grids, commonly associated with retro gaming aesthetics.",
  "description": "Developed within early video game culture as a technical necessity due to hardware limitations, it has evolved into a deliberate artistic choice that embraces the constraints of pixel-based representation.",
  "culturalContext": "Pixel art outlines — selective outline logic in low-resolution grid art — range from 1px \"selective outlining\" (outlining only the side facing light) to hue-shifted colored outlines (purple on skin, never black) that keep forms luminous. The technique (taught in PixelJoint and Lospec tutorials, canonized by Paul Robertson's work) separates professional pixel art from amateur; selective outlining is the medium's signature intelligence.",
